that are preventing them from feeling good about their jobs, but also moving ahead. And then the last two chapters of the book aren't about falling out of love with your job, but about your job not loving you back. And I think for those of us who've ever been a runner-up at work, we struggle with getting a promotion or raise, but we don't know why. That can be a really frustrating experience. And I've learned that often kind of comes down to huge communication gaps between what promoters tell you, or at least they think they're implying what you should be doing to get ahead and what you think you should be doing. They're often misaligned. I end with the underappreciated star. This is the person who brings a lot to the workplace and they get a lot of paths on the back.
正是这些问题阻碍了他们对工作产生良好感觉，同时也阻碍了他们向前迈进。然后，书的最后两章关心的不是你不再爱你的工作，而是你的工作没有回馈你的爱。我想，对于我们这些曾在职场中当过“陪跑者”的人来说，我们在争取升职或加薪时苦苦挣扎，却又不知为何。那可能是一段非常令人沮丧的经历。而我了解到，这通常归结为巨大的沟通鸿沟：晋升决策者告诉你（或者至少他们以为自己在暗示）你应该做些什么才能高升，与你认为自己应该做些什么之间，往往存在错位。最后我以“被低估的明星”结尾。这类人为职场带来了很多贡献，他们也得到了很多鼓励。

