00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:38.000
with that thing, but not underestimating the extent to which your negative emotions around it can actually affect other types of your life that you do really care about. Now, you mentioned in the introduction to the book that you've noticed a shift in recent years, especially post-pandemic and how people were thinking about and talking about their relationship with their work. Can you talk about that and how much of that shift do you think is due to the pandemic? Yes, I think, you know, we first saw this rise of kind of, you know, global unhappiness of onwee, of angst around our careers, rising during the pandemic. But I felt like during the pandemic, it was much easier for us to kind of finger point and blame it on the stress we were feeling from working from home.
是很重要的，但同时也不要低估它所带来的负面情绪实际上会影响到你真正在乎的其他类型的生活。现在，你在书的引言中提到，你注意到近年来发生了一个转变，特别是在大流行之后，人们思考和谈论他们与工作关系的方式发生了变化。你能谈谈这一点吗？你认为这种转变有多少是由大流行引起的？是的，我认为，你知道的，我们最早看到这种全球性不幸福感的兴起，这种对职业的厌倦和焦虑情绪，是在大流行期间开始上升的。但我觉得在大流行期间，我们更容易把矛头指向我们因居家办公而感受到的压力，并将其归咎于此。

17
00:09:38.000 --> 00:10:11.000
The stress of, you know, the global economy catch, just crashing, people sick, you know, that, The pandemic was a very easy sort of blame for people who are experiencing workplace unhappiness. But you fast forward a few years, and here we are, and people are still miserable. Gallup just came out with a poll showing that, you know, 19% of people are just incredibly unhappy at work. But we don't quite have the same sort of source to blame it on. And I think for a lot of people, there's sort of this nebulous feeling that they're miserable and they're not quite sure why.
全球经济崩溃、人们生病等带来的压力，对于经历职场不快的人来说，大流行是一个非常容易找到的替罪羊。但几年过去了，我们现在身处这样的现实中，人们依然痛苦不堪。盖洛普（Gallup）刚刚发布了一项民意调查显示，19% 的人在工作中感到极其痛苦。但我们没有了同样明显的罪魁祸首来归咎。并且我认为，对于许多人来说，有一种模糊的感觉，觉得自己很痛苦，却又说不清原因。

