You hear people sort of use causal language, like once our company merged with another one, everything fell apart, or once we got rid of our in office, our brick and mortar office, communication was no longer good, or my boss moved. So they're grappling with trying to sort of blame their unhappiness on a particular source, but they're not often sort of figuring out that that source is really their own kind of psychological relationship with their career, that it's actually kind of much deeper than just, a new boss or a new office, it isn't so much these structural things, but these kind of deep psychological issues. And that's kind of the shift that I've seen in the last couple years since the pandemic.
你会听到人们使用因果关系的语言，比如“自从我们公司跟另一家公司合并后，一切都崩溃了”，或者“自从我们取消了办公室、实体办公室后，沟通就不好了”，或者“我的老板调走了”。因此，他们费力地试图将自己的不快乐归咎于某个特定的根源，但他们往往没有弄明白，这个根源其实是他们自己对职业的心理关系，其实比单纯的一个新老板或新办公室要深得多，问题不在于这些结构性的东西，而在于这些深层的心理问题。这就是我自大流行以来在过去几年里看到的转变。

